      <h2>Frequently Asked Questions About Sleep Architecture</h2>
      <div class="faq-list">
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>How much deep sleep does a healthy adult need each night?</summary>
          <p>For most healthy adults, Stage N3 Slow-Wave deep sleep accounts for approximately 15% to 20% of total sleep time, which equates to roughly 70 to 100 minutes per night. Deep sleep naturally declines as humans age, dropping from roughly 20% in young adulthood to less than 10% in older adults.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Can you catch up on missed deep sleep during weekend lie-ins?</summary>
          <p>Only partially. When you are sleep-deprived, your body exhibits a homeostatic rebound effect, prioritizing Stage 3 deep sleep during the following night. However, sleeping late on weekends disrupts your circadian phase for the upcoming work week, creating Sunday night insomnia.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Why do smartwatches show different sleep stages compared to medical sleep studies?</summary>
          <p>Commercial fitness wearables estimate sleep stages based on wrist movement (accelerometry) and heart rate variability (optical photoplethysmography). While useful for broad trends, they cannot measure actual EEG brainwave oscillations like a clinical polysomnography test in a sleep laboratory.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Why do I only remember dreams on certain mornings?</summary>
          <p>Dream recall is strongest when you wake up directly out of an active REM sleep period. If you transition from REM into Stage 2 light sleep before waking, the neurochemical memory of the dream rapidly fades within minutes.</p>
        </details>
        <details class="faq-item">
          <summary>Does sleeping in a cold room improve sleep architecture?</summary>
          <p>Yes. The onset of Stage 3 Slow-Wave deep sleep requires your core body temperature to drop by approximately 1 to 2 degrees Fahrenheit. An overly warm bedroom prevents this thermoregulatory decline, causing frequent micro-arousals and shallow sleep.</p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
